 "pitch": {
  "logline": "Two estranged sisters push a dead station wagon across the desert to deliver their mother's last letter.",
  "why": "I spent my summers in a motel my aunt ran at the edge of a dry lake. Nobody stayed more than a night, and everybody told her more than they meant to.\n\nSalt Flat is that place and those people: one road, one motel, two sisters who have run out of ways to avoid each other. I wrote it for what a small crew can shoot well in twelve days.",
  "audience": {
   "primary": "Women over thirty who go to independent films with a sister, a mother or a friend.",
   "secondary": "Anyone who has driven a long way with family and not talked about why.",
   "notes": "A festival first, then the podcasts and newsletters this audience already trusts."
  },
  "sections": [
   {
    "id": "sec_story",
    "heading": "The story",
    "body": "Lena wants the letter burned. June wants it read. Their mother asked for it to be left under a stone on the salt flats, and the only way there is together.\n\nBy the time they reach the stone, the car is the least broken thing they have fixed."
   }
  ],
